From b0fc6a52660d60a64b9ce0bcd7f28276a74ef9dd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jayneelatintel <jayneel.vora@intel.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 08:10:52 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 17/21] Vulkan: single-token Q8_0 flash attention for Intel
 Xe2/Xe3 iGPUs, FA tuning knobs

On Intel the scalar FA shader never groups the query heads of a KV head
when there are 16 of them (max_gqa = block_rows = 8), so every K and V
row is loaded and dequantized 16 times; grouping them
(GGML_VK_FA_GQA_ROWS=16) needs more registers than the compiler has,
and the shader spills.

flash_attn_dec_xe.comp takes one chunk of 128 KV positions of one KV
head per workgroup for all 16 query heads: int8 dot products for the
scores (Q quantized on entry), max and row sums in shared memory, then
P x V in four streams of 32 threads that each own 4 values of all 16
heads. It writes split-k partials for the existing fa_split_k_reduce,
which also applies the sinks.

Used for N == 1, head size 128, Q8_0 K and V, exactly 16 query heads per
KV head, no ALiBi or softcap, on the devices that get the Xe matvec.
GGML_VK_FA_DEC_XE=0 turns it off, GGML_VK_FA_DEC_XE_CHUNK=32/64 changes
the chunk, GGML_VK_FA_DEC_XE_DIAG=1/2/3 skips the score or P x V step
for timing (wrong results).

Knobs for the generic shader, all off by default: GGML_VK_FA_SPLIT_WGS
(split_k target workgroup count), GGML_VK_FA_GQA_ROWS and
GGML_VK_FA_SUBGROUP.

test-backend-ops: the kernel's other paths on the model shape (1 and 4
KV heads, sinks, no mask, two sequences, 40 positions, the cache's
permuted layout). Every FLASH_ATTN_EXT case passes with the kernel on
and off.
